// ============================================================
// CHARACTER: Simon Peter (Restored)
// ACT: Act IV/V
// CASE: The Resurrection Appearance
// CASE ID: restoration
// SOURCE: ORPHAN FILE - Peter's restoration after the resurrection
// BIBLE REFERENCE: John 21:15-19; Luke 24:34; Acts 2:14-47
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPHECIES FULFILLED IN THIS CASE:
//   - Isaiah 54:7-8
//     Gospel: John 21:15-17 — "Though he was angry, I will take my stand with you"
//     Insight: Even in rejection, God's love remains. Peter's restoration was prophetic of Israel's restoration.
//   "I have made you the father of many nations" points to the church built on Peter's testimony.
//   - Genesis 12:2-3
//     Gospel: Matthew 16:18-19 → fulfilled in John 21:15-17
//     Insight: Peter's commission to "feed my sheep" unfolds in Acts as he becomes the foundational apostle.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// BIBLICAL CONTEXT:
//   Summary: After the resurrection, Jesus appeared to Peter by the Sea of Galilee. Three times He asked Peter if he loved Him, restoring their relationship through the threefold denial.
//   Historical Note: This was the third post-resurrection appearance recorded in John's Gospel, where Peter's leadership role was publicly reinstated.
//   Significance: The Great Commission — "Feed my sheep" — shows grace triumphs over failure. Peter would become the rock upon which the church was built.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CASE FACTS (Truth Object):
//   culprit: none
//   motive: Restoration, not retribution — Jesus came to heal the broken, including Peter.
//   method: Three questions about love, each answered with "Feed my sheep" or "Shepherd my flock."
// ============================================================
//

-> start
=== start ===
I saw Him on the beach. We had fished all night and caught nothing. Then He stood there — alive. He spoke to me about love. Three times He asked, and three times I answered.
* [Tell me about the questions.] -> questions
* [What did He say to you?] -> commission
=== questions ===
'Do you love me more than these?' He asked. I said yes. 'Do you love me?' Again, I said yes. 'Do you love me?' A third time — my heart broke. The threefold question for my threefold denial.
* [And your answer?] -> commission
=== commission ===
'Simon, son of John, do you love me?' Yes, Lord. 'Feed my lambs.' Again — 'Do you love me?' Yes, Lord. 'Feed my sheep.' The third time — 'Lord, you know all things. You know that I love you.' 'Feed my sheep.'
* [The Great Commission?] -> closing
=== closing ===
He forgave me. Not with words of reproach, but with a calling. 'Follow me.' I am the rock that was shattered and made whole. The church would be built on this restored love.
-> DONE